    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f67b2645ba61f0cc968f7d94b5cffac7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "Industry Ministry-UNDP to establish expert unit to support industrial reforms\n\nThe Industry and Entrepreneurship Development Ministry and United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) have partnered to establish a specialised unit to strengthen the government\u2019s technical capacity in implementing industrial and entrepreneurship reforms.\n\nThe Expert Support Unit will be embedded within the ministry and will provide technical, analytical and coordination support across areas including small and medium enterprise development, industrial zones, trade policy, digital transformation, data analytics, skills development and international relations.\n\nA memorandum of understanding was signed by Ministry Secretary J.M. Thilaka Jayasundara and UNDP Sri Lanka Resident Representative Azusa Kubota, in the presence of Deputy Minister Chathuranga Abeysinghe.\n\nThe multidisciplinary unit is expected to support policy formulation, programme implementation, project management, monitoring and performance measurement. It will also facilitate the deployment of technical specialists and seek support from the development partners and private sector.\n\nHowever, the ministry and UNDP did not disclose the proposed size of the unit, its funding requirement or a timeline for it to become operational.\n\nAbeysinghe said that Sri Lanka\u2019s industrial growth, entrepreneurship and export ambitions required the public institutions to be equipped with specialised expertise and more agile approaches.\n\n\u201cThe Expert Support Unit will help us accelerate key priorities while strengthening the ministry\u2019s ability to respond effectively to the emerging challenges and opportunities,\u201d he said.\n\nThe initiative comes as the government seeks to improve SME competitiveness, strengthen export-oriented industries and expand Sri Lanka\u2019s participation in global value chains."
  },
