    "description": "Why Sri Lanka needs to protect its remaining elephants",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Why-Sri-Lanka-needs-to-protect-its-remaining-elephants/172-348018",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786568779.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-43be63a4-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9cae9fc8c8d7d3824c13cb2928a4b5d5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1556,
    "chunk_text": "Why Sri Lanka needs to protect its remaining elephants\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s elephant problem is far from over. With each passing day, the gentle giants of our jungles are facing a worrisome plight. Statistics indicate that over 350 elephants had succumbed to injuries as a result of the human-elephant conflict. But this conflict, if managed properly, could have been averted.\n\nIt is a known fact that farmers and people of yesteryear coexisted with nature. Farmers kept a portion of their harvest (known as Kurulu Paluwa) for birds and animals such as elephants. This was an unharvested outer strip or buffer zone of the paddy field left deliberately near forest boundaries. During the early 20th century, Sri Lanka was mostly covered by dense natural forests. These dense forests allowed large herds of elephants to roam around freely. Due to low population density, small, scattered rural settlements left wide, uninterrupted migratory pathways for elephant herds. Old agricultural practices used low-impact methods that didn\u2019t permanently block elephant ranges.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s tank cascade system especially in the dry zone has been recognised by the Food and Agricultural Organisation (FAO) as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage system. It included forest patches surrounding main reservoirs which collected rainwater to be used during the dry season. These forest patches and peripheral tree belts provided shade and the necessary food for elephants. But as the population grew, and people were in need of land, these forest patches were cleared."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
