    "language": "en",
    "md5": "954f33065eb650242f3ee75735da7a12",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1856,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s tank cascade system especially in the dry zone has been recognised by the Food and Agricultural Organisation (FAO) as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage system. It included forest patches surrounding main reservoirs which collected rainwater to be used during the dry season. These forest patches and peripheral tree belts provided shade and the necessary food for elephants. But as the population grew, and people were in need of land, these forest patches were cleared.\n\nAround 1950s and 1960s, the then governments considered putting up electric fences to confine elephants to certain regions. In 1998, the first community-based elephant fence was erected by the Sri Lanka Wildlife Conservation Society in Pussellayaya. However, over the years, many of these fences were defunct\u00a0 due to poor maintenance and management, and elephant fences weren\u2019t identified as a feasible solution.\n\nToday, people in rural areas consider elephants as a menace. They go to any length to keep elephants away. Illegal electric fences have taken away the lives of many elephants such as Barana, the iconic tusker that roamed around Kala Wewa. The use of fire crackers, elephant crackers, snaring have pushed elephants towards more vulnerabilities. Farmers are compelled to aim their firearms at elephants who come to their vegetable plots and as a result many wild elephants succumb to gunshot injuries.\n\nSri Lanka has witnessed numerous infrastructure developments that ultimately took a toll on the environment. Construction of road networks via elephant migratory routes, constructing commercial establishments and human settlements along elephant corridors have aggravated the conflict further. It was very much later that government officials realised the need to have wildlife underpasses at least as a pilot project to mitigate the conflict."
  },
