    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-d7b7c970-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "17a17479e6810c1886134c9913b755bf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "On the same day (Monday, August 10) the Cardinal released the statement informing the Catholic Church\u2019s stance on capital punishment, interestingly,\u00a0 the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government announced that it considers implementing the death penalty for drug-related offences as part of a tougher response to the growing threat posed by narcotics.\n\nThe question of reimplementing the death penalty in Sri Lanka comes to the fore occasionally when a brutal murder takes place, such as the rape and killing of five-year-old Seya Sadevmi of Kotadeniyawa,\u00a0 and 18-year-old schoolgirl Sivaloganathan Vithiya of Pungudutivu, both in 2015. However, every time the discussion has been suppressed by the opposition,\u00a0 by those who claim to be concerned about human rights,\u00a0 and those who\u00a0 really see the practical problems in reimplementing it.\n\nThe last judicial execution in Sri Lanka was carried out on June 23, 1976, at Welikada Prison, when convicted criminal Embilipitiye Chandradasa, also known as \u201cHonda Papuwa\u201d, was hanged. Consequently, on 22 May 1977, the fifth anniversary of the Republic, President William Gopallawa commuted the sentences of everyone on death row: 144 men and 3 women. Thereafter, all executive Presidents too,\u00a0 commuted every sentence of death.\n\nVeteran legal expert Dr. Nihal Jayawickrama who served then as the Permanent Secretary to the Ministry of Justice argues that there are no prisoners on death row now according to a procedure prescribed in a justice ministry standing order introduced when Mr. Felix Dias Bandaranaike was Minister of Justice. \u201cIf either the trial Judge or the Attorney General had recommended that the sentence should not be carried out, the Minister advised that the sentence be commuted to one of life imprisonment,\u201d he points out,\u00a0 and contends that a prisoner serving a life sentence cannot now be hanged. This is what has hitherto happened."
  },
