    "language": "en",
    "md5": "8e986197644d9ca0684147a80b1d95fa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1754,
    "chunk_text": "Over 10,000 young men and women, mostly innocent people,\u00a0 were killed by the armed forces and the police during the first insurrection of the JVP in 1971, while the JVP on its part also took away the right of life of\u00a0 thousands of those seen by them as government informants. The number of extra judicial killings in the south by the armed forces and\u00a0 vigilante groups - PRRA and Green Tigers - during the second insurrection of the JVP was placed at more than 60.000. One wonders if the\u00a0 JVP could\u00a0 have had such a large number of armed fighters.\n\nThe best case in point for the hypocrisy of the international community that rejects any kind of deprivation of right to life is the ongoing genocide in Gaza.\n\nOn the other hand, even if we accept\u00a0 capital punishment as a deterrent to certain crimes, the inevitable question that challenges the implementation of it is whether we have\u00a0 an untainted law enforcement and justice system. The media occasionally carries out allegations of introducing drugs into vehicles, homes, or personal properties of innocent people by corrupt police officers. It must be remembered that the police had extracted a \u201cconfession\u201d of rape and murder of Seya Sadewmi from a person whom they nicknamed \u201cKondaya,\u201d before they arrested the real murderer.\n\nHence, the danger in carrying out the death sentence, even in the event of its acceptability being established, is the possibility of wrong people being sent to the gallows on planted or manufactured evidence. In a casual search in free Wikipedia, several pages were found that have catalogued a large number of such miscarriages of justice around the world in which some unfortunate men had been exonerated decades after they were executed or incarcerated."
  },
