    "date_str": "2026-08-13",
    "description": "The iconic MiG-21:  Interceptor which gave third world air forces a fighting chance",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/The-iconic-MiG-21-Interceptor-which-gave-third-world-air-forces-a-fighting-chance/172-348021",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786569836.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-bfc8add5-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a9265b167d0ef0906ebe747f60d2898f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "The iconic MiG-21:  Interceptor which gave third world air forces a fighting chance\n\nOn July 7 1966, a sleek, fast fighter plane hitherto un-encountered by Western air forces came like a bolt from the blue to down an American F-105 Thunderchief fighter bomber over North Vietnam, marking the first-ever MiG-21 aerial victory and forcing US to re-think air warfare tactics.\n\nBut, while the Republic of Vietnam retired its MiG-21 fleet a decade ago in 2015, India retained its faith in this iconic Cold-War era interceptor, finally\u00a0 retiring its MiG-21 fighters only\u00a0 in September 2025, after 62 years of active service.\n\nThe Mikoyan-Guryevich MiG-21, produced in greater numbers than any other supersonic aircraft in aviation history, still continues to serve with several air forces including Cuba, Angola and North Korea, testimony to its adaptability and durability.\n\nThe MiG-21 Bison (its NATO codename) entered service with the Indian Air Force (IAF) in 1963,\u00a0 when\u00a0 \u00a0North Vietnam\u2019s fledgling air force was still in the making, and pilots were sent to China and the ex-USSR for training. They would have to combat formidable American\u00a0 opponents such as the McDonnell Douglas F-4 Phantom II and the\u00a0 F-105 Thunderchief, the backbone of the U.S. Air Force\u2019s tactical bombing operations during the early and middle years of the Vietnam War.\n\nThe Americans believed the Phantom to be invincible in the air. It was a twin-engined, two seater multi-role intercepter, and its designers were so confident of the potency of its\u00a0 twin Sidewinder air-to-air missiles\u00a0 that they did away with the traditional Canon armament altogether. It was also believed that the small, newly formed North Vietnamese air force, equipped initially with subsonic MiG 17 interceptors, would be no serious threat\u00a0 for the Phantom.\n\nNorth Vietnam did not have any jet fighters\u00a0 prior to 1964. In early 1964, Hanoi received 36 subsonic MiG-15 trainers\u00a0 and MiG-17s."
  },
