    "chunk_size_bytes": 1899,
    "chunk_text": "The Americans believed the Phantom to be invincible in the air. It was a twin-engined, two seater multi-role intercepter, and its designers were so confident of the potency of its\u00a0 twin Sidewinder air-to-air missiles\u00a0 that they did away with the traditional Canon armament altogether. It was also believed that the small, newly formed North Vietnamese air force, equipped initially with subsonic MiG 17 interceptors, would be no serious threat\u00a0 for the Phantom.\n\nNorth Vietnam did not have any jet fighters\u00a0 prior to 1964. In early 1964, Hanoi received 36 subsonic MiG-15 trainers\u00a0 and MiG-17s.\n\nThe first MiG-21s arrived in Vietnam in 1965. It was much lighter than the Phantom and had a shorter range. The Mikoyan-Guryevich Design Bureau had designed the\u00a0 MiG-21 for very short ground-controlled interception (GCI) missions. It became renowned for this type of mission over the skies over North Vietnam.\n\nTogether with the already proven MiG 17s, they performed \u201cguerrilla warfare in the air\u201d with the MiG-17s flying below radar and appearing like ghosts from below American formations,\u00a0 while the MiG-21s attacked from the rear. These tactics were so effective that the F-105 Thunderchiefs which flew over 20,000 combat missions over Vietnam was finally withdrawn from service.\n\nAlthough 13 of North Vietnam\u2019s flying aces flew the MiG-21,\u00a0 many North Vietnamese People\u2019s Air Force (NVPA) pilots preferred the MiG-17 because the high wing loading of the MiG-21 made it relatively less maneuverable,\u00a0 and the\u00a0 canopy of the MiG-17 gave better visibility.\n\nBut\u00a0 British author Roger Boniface who\u00a0 interviewed NVPA aces Pham Ngoc Lan and\u00a0 Nguyn Nht Chi\u00eau (who scored victories in both MiG 17s and 21s) was told by Pham Ngoc Lan\u00a0 that \u201cThe MiG-21 was much faster, and it had two ATOLL missiles which were very accurate and reliable when fired between 1,000 and 1,200 yards.\u201d"
