    "md5": "91690350daba9a13e4a0acaee4c60d81",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1891,
    "chunk_text": "Inspired by the Buddhist revival led by Anagarika Dharmapala, he began using the name \u2018Gunapala Piyasena\u2019 in place of his foreign names, \u2018George\u2019 and \u2018Pieris\u2019, and subsequently became known as G. P. Malalasekera. Subsequently, he joined the staff of Ananda College, Colombo, and, through successive promotions, served as the college\u2019s Deputy Principal and Acting Principal. Later, he was appointed as the first registered Principal of Nalanda College, Colombo, and elevated the institution to a prominent position within the country\u2019s education system.\n\nHis research into Buddhism and Buddhist civilisation was extensive in scope; consequently, he became the Editor-in-Chief of the Encyclopaedia of Buddhism. Elected as Secretary of the All Ceylon Buddhist Congress in 1927, he subsequently served as its Vice-President and President, dedicating himself to an unceasing mission of service. Driven by a deep love for his country and nation, he further expanded his contributions by serving as an active member of the Royal Asiatic Society of Ceylon.\n\nMalalasekera\u2019s hallmark\u2014or his most distinctive publication\u2014was the \u2018Malalasekera Sinhala-English Dictionary\u2019 (1948). It was a magnificent contribution to the linguistic and scholarly heritage of the nation. This work garnered attention and acclaim not only within the country but across the globe. He also represented Sri Lanka at various international conferences, including those held in England, Hawaii, India, Pakistan, and the United States. Furthermore, he held the presidency of the World Fellowship of Buddhists and the Ceylon Arts Society with great distinction.\n\nProfessor Malalasekera served with distinguished diplomatic skill as Ceylon\u2019s High Commissioner to Canada, as Ceylon\u2019s Permanent Representative to the United Nations in New York, and as Ceylon\u2019s High Commissioner to the United Kingdom."
  },
