    "chunk_size_bytes": 1099,
    "chunk_text": "Secondly, it is critical to renew national consultations to legalise abortion with key stakeholders and draft legal amendments based on recent empirical evidence. Although abortion is illegal in Sri Lanka, both the law and enforcement practices send mixed signals. For instance, while abortion is prohibited and carries severe penalties, authorities often turn a blind eye to operational clinics that provide such services. Furthermore, women are immune from prosecution and are encouraged to seek treatment for complications arising from abortions, recognising the adverse effects on maternal mortality as well as physical and psychological health.\n\nFurthermore, any significant discussion on proposing amendments to the criminal law to legalise abortion must involve all key stakeholders, including representatives of women and civil society organisations that advocate for safe abortion. Excluding women, whose rights are central to the debate, undermines their fundamental right to equality and health and their agency to effect change for themselves and foster social transformation for others."
