    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3b553f1075d5ddc86e29842d79c4c235",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1938,
    "chunk_text": "A Pyrrhic Triumph on the Bench\n\nIn politics, there are victories, but not all of them carry positive consequences in the long run. In contrast, victories entail disastrous consequences to the winner. It means victory carries the bitter taste of defeat.\n\nThe government\u2019s arrogance in pushing forward with constitutional amendments to extend the mandatory retirement age of superior court judges\u2014raising Supreme Court tenure to 67 and the Court of Appeal to 65\u2014falls squarely into the latter category. Even if this law successfully navigates the parliamentary process, it will stand as a classic Pyrrhic victory: a hard-fought battle won on paper, but at the catastrophic expense of public confidence, the morale of the wider legal fraternity, and the sacrosanct global principles of good governance.\n\nAt its core, statecraft requires not merely the raw exercise of parliamentary majority, but the preservation of moral authority. The use of numerical strength to bulldoze its way is not statecraft at all.\u00a0 Judicial independence is sacrosanct, and the mere public mandate should not be used as a trump card to subvert it in the interests of the ruling side.\n\nWhen the government\u00a0 chooses to press ahead with structural tenure changes despite the unanimous resistance from\u00a0 \u00a0the Bar Association of Sri Lanka (BASL), regional bodies like the Commonwealth Lawyers Association, and mounting civil disquiet, it signals a dangerous detachment from democratic consensus.\n\nThe government attempts to frame this contentious intervention as a move to retain expertise in the judiciary for dispensing with a backlog of cases. Officials point to the staggering backlog of over a million pending cases across the nation\u2019s court system, arguing that retaining experienced judicial minds is the need of the hour. No one denies the severity of the crisis plaguing our courts. Systemic backlogs degrade the fundamental right to speedy justice."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-0b55fae7",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-0b55fae7",
    "date_str": "2026-08-13",
    "description": "A Pyrrhic Triumph on the Bench",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/A-Pyrrhic-Triumph-on-the-Bench/172-348077",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786641954.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-13-dailymirrorlk-0b55fae7-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "36ebe2ef8b7083f45725b9f395f48b7c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1716,
    "chunk_text": "The government attempts to frame this contentious intervention as a move to retain expertise in the judiciary for dispensing with a backlog of cases. Officials point to the staggering backlog of over a million pending cases across the nation\u2019s court system, arguing that retaining experienced judicial minds is the need of the hour. No one denies the severity of the crisis plaguing our courts. Systemic backlogs degrade the fundamental right to speedy justice.\n\nGlobal principles of good governance, articulated across the Commonwealth Latimer House Principles and international jurisprudence, emphasise a cardinal truth: justice must not only be done, but must manifestly and undoubtedly be seen to be done.\n\nJudicial independence is fragile. It rests not on brick and mortar, but entirely on public trust. When tenure rules for active superior court judges are modified by the political branch, it inevitably invites public suspicion.\n\nThis policy creates internal fractures within the judicial hierarchy itself. As associations representing district judges and magistrates have warned, artificially freezing the apex of the legal system creates a logistical and psychological bottleneck. Promotion pathways stall, career progression for younger and ambitious legal minds is choked, and institutional stagnation trickles down from the top. If the administration forces this law through parliament, it may successfully alter the numerical age limits written into our constitutional text. But what will it have gained? It will have alienated the legal profession, darkened the window of institutional transparency, rattled public faith in impartial adjudication, and violated the spirit of cooperative governance."
  },
