    "chunk_size_bytes": 1385,
    "chunk_text": "This policy creates internal fractures within the judicial hierarchy itself. As associations representing district judges and magistrates have warned, artificially freezing the apex of the legal system creates a logistical and psychological bottleneck. Promotion pathways stall, career progression for younger and ambitious legal minds is choked, and institutional stagnation trickles down from the top. If the administration forces this law through parliament, it may successfully alter the numerical age limits written into our constitutional text. But what will it have gained? It will have alienated the legal profession, darkened the window of institutional transparency, rattled public faith in impartial adjudication, and violated the spirit of cooperative governance.\n\nIt is still not too late for the government to take a step back and shelve the enactment of the proposed piece of legislation. Otherwise, it will be a pyrrhic triumph. The alienation of legal fraternity, a powerful voice and a lobby, does not bode well for any government in a democratic set-up.\u00a0 \u00a0In Sri Lanka, there are examples from the past.\u00a0 The impeachment of then Chief Justice Shirani Bandaranayake turned out to be a pyrrhic victory for the Mahinda Rajapaksa government.\u00a0 The incident event create internal rifts in that government with its leftist allies having dissension over the impeachment."
