    "chunk_size_bytes": 1438,
    "chunk_text": "Beyond the licensing dispute, the audit and regulatory records point to compliance issues at individual zones. In the Kandy Industrial Park, authorities flagged a factory over the quality of its treated wastewater, compounded by an expired environmental license; the BOI said the park\u2019s zonal EPL \u201cwill also be considered accordingly\u201d once the factory secures a renewal.\n\nThe Wagawaththa Industrial Zone continues to operate without a centralised wastewater treatment plant altogether; while individual factories manage their own waste, the BOI said it \u201csubmitted a request for an EPL for the zone by letter dated 02.08.2024,\u201d but \u201cno response has been received from the CEA to date.\u201d\n\nIn the Mirijjawila Export Processing Zone, a dormant company was repeatedly instructed to obtain a Scheduled Waste Management License, which it secured only in March 2025.\n\nThese compliance gaps carry particular weight given that Biyagama and Seethawaka sit within the Kelani River basin, the source of approximately 80 per cent of Colombo\u2019s drinking water supply. CEA records show that high-risk industrial discharges into the river require evaluation by an inter-agency High and Medium Polluting Industry Committee, which assessed 31 industrial proposals in 2023. Separate CEA inspections along a 500-metre buffer zone of the river identified 86 high-risk chemical, polymer, and waste-handling facilities requiring ongoing monitoring."
