    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1ee98c21680cace43487e254108f41d4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "The NPP Government has allocated funds for the latest phase of the investigation and the Justice Ministry has said international forensic assistance will be sought for DNA identification. The administration has also pumped additional money into the Office on Missing Persons (OMP) and says it intends to establish a Truth and Reconciliation Commission (TRC).\n\nThe NPP Government has inherited a transitional justice cemetery containing rather more promises than results. The OMP has existed for a decade but its credibility among victim communities remains badly damaged. Sri Lanka\u2019s history of special presidential and parliamentary commissions of inquiry is almost a genre unto itself. And trust is a vital currency of any transitional justice program.\n\nThus the proposed TRC will mean little if it becomes yet another elegant bureaucratic receptacle into which inconvenient history can be poured and forgotten but for those who keep sipping the holy grail of transitional justice. And the NPP will through no fault of its own have to work doubly hard to win or earn the trust of the families of the dead or disappeared under a previous dispensation. For increasing a budget does not automatically boost a project\u2019s legitimacy.\n\nNor has the Government yet resolved the fundamental contradiction represented by the long lived and still much dreaded Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA). The NPP faithfully promised its repeal, but the replacement legislation has itself attracted criticism for retaining sweeping powers of arrest and detention. And the PTA continues to hang over the heads of dissenters, political opponents and minor societal nuisances like a Damocles\u2019 sword.\n\nThe Government\u2019s rejection of the UN\u2019s external evidence-gathering mechanism remains a serious limitation on its claim to be embracing the fullest possible accountability, meanwhile. And this is where the phrase \u2018reconciliation\u2019 needs rescuing from political upholstery."
  },
  {
