    "md5": "c7b5a60d69036593658afbbef41ac945",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "Decriminalising LGBTIQ, death penalty etc. as means to secure GSP+\n\nCriminalisation of same-sex relations was introduced into our countries by the British\n\nThe European Union (EU),\u00a0 it is reported, has called on Lanka to adopt comprehensive anti-discrimination laws to protect LGBTIQ persons as well as for decriminalisation of same sex relations as a means to achieve GST+ benefits. Wow!\u00a0 Same sex relationships and fluid gender expressions are widely acknowledged and recorded in regional literature. They were accommodated within pre-colonial South Asian societies. As for the death sentence,\u00a0 it is a dead letter.\n\nCriminalisation of same-sex relations was introduced into our countries by the British\u00a0 \u00a0who introduced Section 377of the Penal Code in1860 (enacted in 1862) across British India. It later served as the legal template for criminalising same-sex acts in neighbouring territories like Sri Lanka and Pakistan. Today, the EU calls on Lanka to decriminalise what was never looked on as a criminal act prior to Europeans inflicting their narrow-minded beliefs into our part of the world.\n\nAt a given time in history, even with all economic odds stacked against it, the then Sri Lankan governments dared challenge the great economic powers of the day. The then government proceeded to do what it felt was best for the interests of this country. For instance, it began taking back its assets from the colonial powers.\n\nIt nationalised the tea estates, took over distribution of petroleum products and even closed down the Israeli embassy in the country in 1971. This despite pro-Israel groups in the United Kingdom threatening to boycott Ceylon tea if Lanka persisted with the move.\n\nAt that time,\u00a0 the export of tea was Lanka\u2019s biggest exchange earner. But the then government proceeded with the closure of the Embassy regardless of threats."
  },
