    "description": "A solution to the drug-fuelled prison system How the rehabilitation of 12,000 LTTE cadres can show the way",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/A-solution-to-the-drug-fuelled-prison-system-How-the-rehabilitation-of-12-000-LTTE-cadres-can-show-the-way/172-348152",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786739749.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-15-dailymirrorlk-f43c8a23-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "027b1067e109e949d66680e7b7a352c9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1806,
    "chunk_text": "A solution to the drug-fuelled prison system How the rehabilitation of 12,000 LTTE cadres can show the way\n\nSri Lanka undertook a mass rehabilitation and reintegration of 12, 191 surrendered or captured LTTE cadres successfully (Photo by Kushan Pathiraja).\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s prison system \u2013 and the judiciary \u2013 that keep flooding overcrowded prisons with an endless stream of inmates is a worthy case study of what one would call systemic incompetence.\n\nThanks to the accumulated rot, the prison system is now exploding in violent riots with alarming frequency, reminiscent of a Latin American narco-state. The crisis in the prison system is what successive governments in the past and an inept bureaucracy made it to be. The same quarters are now blaming a \u2018conspiracy\u2019 behind the riots in overcrowded prison cells that house four times the number of people they were meant to accommodate. And things cannot be\u00a0 grotesquely comical and out of touch when a supposedly advisory body \u2013 the Country Together national operation council, chaired by the president himself \u2013 explores the death sentence for drug offences.\n\nThe crisis in the prison system is not beyond simple commonsense fixes. But indifference and the frequent tendency for piecemeal solutions have now led the system to its dangerous breaking point.\n\nFirst consider the crisis in numbers. Sri Lankan prison system, which was built to accommodate only 11,000 inmates, housed 39,516 as of this month.\n\nNearly three-quarters of the prison population (28,646)\u00a0 are pre-trial detainees. There are only 10,870 convicted inmates. The numbers become even more despairing as a Sunday Times investigation revealed that historically over 50%-68% of annual convicted admissions into the prison system are petty offenders jailed for unpaid fines."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-15-dailymirrorlk-f43c8a23",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f43c8a23",
    "date_str": "2026-08-15",
    "description": "A solution to the drug-fuelled prison system How the rehabilitation of 12,000 LTTE cadres can show the way",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/A-solution-to-the-drug-fuelled-prison-system-How-the-rehabilitation-of-12-000-LTTE-cadres-can-show-the-way/172-348152",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1786739749.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-15-dailymirrorlk-f43c8a23-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3cef49d5916659b4af5b546a8906a7ae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1787,
    "chunk_text": "Nearly three-quarters of the prison population (28,646)\u00a0 are pre-trial detainees. There are only 10,870 convicted inmates. The numbers become even more despairing as a Sunday Times investigation revealed that historically over 50%-68% of annual convicted admissions into the prison system are petty offenders jailed for unpaid fines.\n\nThe police had recently spent an inordinate amount of time and resources to lock up drug addicts as part of the government\u2019s anti-drug campaign. The prison population, 28,000 in 2024 swelled to 40,000 as a result of the influx of drug addicts.\n\nHowever, the sense of urgency in locking up the suspects is not seen in the legal process.\n\nThe government\u2019s analyst department is a byword for inefficiency. Remand inmates are held in prolonged detention owing to the inefficiency in the system, awaiting test reports, and ineffectual judges who have failed to use the full course of legal provisions to grant bail.\n\nThen, inadequacy of rehabilitation has turned the prison system into a revolving door for drug offenders. Only a fraction of the convicted drug offenders go through some sort of rehabilitation programme in prison. Of nearly 5,000 convicted drug offenders in the prison system, only 1,506 are currently undergoing formal rehabilitation.\u00a0 The remainder, 3,456 inmates, are housed in normal wards due to the lack of facilities.\n\nThe solution to Sri Lanka\u2019s prison crisis is not rocket science.\n\nThe justice minister has proposed state-funded bail banks to support inmates held due to unpaid fines. That is the right approach. But a more practical long-term remedy would be unpaid fine-related prison sentences to be converted to mandatory community service. That would halve the number of convicted prisoners held in the prison system."
  },
