    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d71097b99c08abf356eb5eb2d90b6e7c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "The justice minister has proposed state-funded bail banks to support inmates held due to unpaid fines. That is the right approach. But a more practical long-term remedy would be unpaid fine-related prison sentences to be converted to mandatory community service. That would halve the number of convicted prisoners held in the prison system.\n\nHowever, considering that the vast majority of pre-trial detainees are drug addicts, a house arrest system could itself become a revolving door. Therefore, instead of protracted court proceedings, the government should devise a simpler mandatory rehabilitation programme for drug addicts.\n\nThat is indeed a compassionate alternative to locking up drug addicts in prisons. Officials might find an excuse in the lack of rehabilitation facilities and trained counsellors.\n\nHowever, the government can borrow from the experience of one of the most successful rehabilitation exercises in the past. In the aftermath of the war, Sri Lanka undertook a mass rehabilitation and reintegration of 12, 191 surrendered or captured LTTE cadres.\n\nThat is one of the most successful deradicalisation and rehabilitation programmes in the world. Nearly two decades since then, there is zero recidivism.\n\nThe programme was handled by the specially created Bureau of the Commissioner General of Rehabilitation \u2013 the government can establish a similar structure for a mass drug rehabilitation initiative.\u00a0 It does not necessarily need to be headed by the military top brass,\u00a0 but the facilities, organisational competency, and fast expertise of the defence forces should be utilised within a civilian-led initiative. The rehabilitation of LTTE cadres lasted 12 to 18 months, and included mandatory counselling, post-traumatic stress therapy and vocational training of\u00a0 40 technical and vocational skills, including IT, carpentry, masonry, tailoring, plumbing and agriculture."
