    "md5": "c31f41bad558a6a1a69dd1e3217b7046",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1819,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka needs modern fishing harbours, proper ice and cold storage facilities and better transport systems to get fish from the harbour to markets around the country. Our fishermen also need access to affordable loans to purchase better boats and equipment. They need modern navigation systems, weather information and technology. The government has already recognised the importance of technology in fisheries. The 2026 Budget allocated Rs. 100 million to develop a system to identify fishing grounds. This is a good step, but much more needs to be done.\n\nThe continuing problem of Indian fishermen entering Sri Lankan waters cannot be ignored. Sri Lanka has repeatedly arrested Indian fishermen who cross into Sri Lankan waters. In the latest incident, nine Indian fishermen were arrested near Talaimannar for allegedly entering Sri Lankan waters and engaging in illegal fishing.\u00a0 According to figures provided by the Indian Government to its Parliament, Sri Lanka has arrested more than 1,300 Indian fishermen since 2023 and seized hundreds of fishing boats. Sri Lanka must protect its territorial waters. This is not about being hostile towards India or Indian fishermen.\n\nThe issue of bottom trawling is even more serious. Bottom trawling involves dragging heavy fishing nets across the seabed. It can destroy marine habitats and damage the areas where fish breed and grow. Sri Lanka banned bottom trawling in 2017, yet incursions by Indian trawlers continue to be reported. The government therefore needs to take a firm position on illegal fishing and continue discussions with India to find a lasting solution. Arresting fishermen every few weeks and then releasing them does not solve the problem. At the same time, Sri Lanka must not use the Indian fishing issue as an excuse for ignoring its own fishermen."
  },
