    "chunk_size_bytes": 1218,
    "chunk_text": "The issue of bottom trawling is even more serious. Bottom trawling involves dragging heavy fishing nets across the seabed. It can destroy marine habitats and damage the areas where fish breed and grow. Sri Lanka banned bottom trawling in 2017, yet incursions by Indian trawlers continue to be reported. The government therefore needs to take a firm position on illegal fishing and continue discussions with India to find a lasting solution. Arresting fishermen every few weeks and then releasing them does not solve the problem. At the same time, Sri Lanka must not use the Indian fishing issue as an excuse for ignoring its own fishermen.\n\nSri Lanka already exports seafood, particularly tuna. With proper investment, there is no reason why the country cannot become a much stronger seafood exporter. This requires political will. The government should set clear targets to reduce fish imports every year while increasing local production. Most importantly, fisheries must be treated as a major part of Sri Lanka\u2019s economy.\n\nOur sea should be feeding our people, supporting our fishermen and strengthening our economy. It should be a warning that we are failing to make use of one of our greatest natural resources."
