    "md5": "3f8b6d5843bceefe7901fc25becf8c5f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1844,
    "chunk_text": "Did Navy craft hit sunken wreck of Cyprus-flagged vessel off Angulana?\n\nColombo, Aug. 17 (Daily Mirror) - Salvage preparations are underway to recover the Sri Lanka Navy Fast Attack Craft that sank off Angulana, as a special board of experts appointed by the Navy Commander begins a formal investigation into the incident, the Navy said.\n\nThe Navy has located the capsized vessel and launched a major salvage operation to bring the craft to the surface before it is towed to the Colombo Port. However, rough sea conditions pose challenges to the recovery operation.\n\nMeanwhile, search operations were continuing until yesterday afternoon for a missing Sri Lanka Navy sailor following the incident, which occurred at around 11:40 a.m. on Saturday when the Fast Attack Craft (FAC) encountered severe difficulties while returning to Colombo after a routine patrol.\n\nEyewitnesses reported seeing the vessel apparently collide with a submerged object, after which smoke was seen coming from the craft before it sank within minutes. However, the exact cause of the incident has not yet been officially confirmed.\n\nIt was reported that, authorities and maritime experts are examining whether the Sri Lanka Navy Fast Attack Craft may have collided with the long-abandoned, Cyprus-flagged merchant vessel 'MV Thermopylae Sierra'. The 155-meter ship had been stranded in local waters off Panadura since 2009 due to pending court litigation before it ultimately sank following years of severe neglect, heavy corrosion, and structural deterioration. Given that the wreckage rested submerged and largely unlit close to the coastline\u2014the exact operational zone where the naval craft met with tragedy\u2014investigators are probing whether the FAC struck the hazardous, ghost-like wreck during its routine patrol, leading to the sudden and catastrophic impact."
