    "chunk_text": "It was reported that, authorities and maritime experts are examining whether the Sri Lanka Navy Fast Attack Craft may have collided with the long-abandoned, Cyprus-flagged merchant vessel 'MV Thermopylae Sierra'. The 155-meter ship had been stranded in local waters off Panadura since 2009 due to pending court litigation before it ultimately sank following years of severe neglect, heavy corrosion, and structural deterioration. Given that the wreckage rested submerged and largely unlit close to the coastline\u2014the exact operational zone where the naval craft met with tragedy\u2014investigators are probing whether the FAC struck the hazardous, ghost-like wreck during its routine patrol, leading to the sudden and catastrophic impact.\n\nOperating as the primary maritime administration, the Director General acts as the Principal Receiver of Wrecks, commanding investigations, managing salvage operations, and enforcing pollution mitigation under international MARPOL standards when accidents occur in local waters.\n\nA joint response involving the Sri Lanka Navy, the Sri Lanka Air Force and local fishermen led to the rescue of 11 of the 12 naval personnel who were on board.\n\nNavy Spokesman Commander Chaminda Walakuluge said the rescued sailors are currently receiving treatment at the Welisala Navy Hospital and that none of them are in critical condition.\n\nThe search for the missing naval officer is continuing alongside the salvage preparations, with authorities closely monitoring the rough sea conditions.\n\nThe special board of experts appointed by the Commander of the Navy will investigate the circumstances surrounding the incident and determine the exact cause of the vessel\u2019s sinking.\n\nEleven rescued after fast attack craft runs into distress off Angulana"
