    "chunk_text": "Drafting in the Dark: Citizens denied a seat at budget table\n\nColombo, Aug. 17 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka is making notable progress in publishing fiscal data, yet ordinary citizens remain almost entirely locked out of shaping how public money is spent. According to the Open Budget Survey 2025, the island scored an encouraging 43 out of 100 for transparency, but a dismal 9 out of 100 for public participation. As preparations for the 2027 budget gather momentum, the government faces a critical test: moving beyond internal ministerial consultations to grant civil society a meaningful voice.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s budget got more transparent this year. The public still has almost no say in it.\n\nThat\u2019s the takeaway from the Open Budget Survey 2025, the International Budget Partnership\u2019s latest assessment of how governments around the world handle public money. The survey scored 82 countries on three separate measures: how much budget information they publish, how well parliament and auditors check government spending, and how much room ordinary citizens get to shape decisions before they\u2019re made. Sri Lanka\u2019s participation score landed at 9 out of 100.\n\nThis isn\u2019t about whether people can read the budget once it\u2019s approved. The participation pillar asks whether anyone outside government gets to shape the budget before it\u2019s finalized: public hearings on spending proposals, formal consultation processes, a way for citizens to comment on what auditors find. It\u2019s the difference between a government that publishes documents and one that actually listens.\n\nSri Lanka does very little of this. Parliament doesn\u2019t take public testimony on the budget or on audit reports. There\u2019s no standing mechanism for civil society or ordinary citizens to feed into how spending decisions get made. The National Audit Office comes closest to offering an entry point, rated as providing \u201cadequate\u201d oversight, but it isn\u2019t really built as a public participation channel either."
  },
