    "description": "Govt. eyes greater defence personnel labour contribution to construction sector",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/Govt-eyes-greater-defence-personnel-labour-contribution-to-construction-sector/56-796160",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1787031855.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-08-18-dailyftlk-4579bd68-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4f62d381c43eab2a93dff624af700fa4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. eyes greater defence personnel labour contribution to construction sector\n\nThe Government is considering a program to encourage greater labour contribution from defence personnel engaged in the construction sector, as part of discussions on priorities and resource allocation for 2027.\n\nThe proposal was discussed at the Defence Ministry\u2019s 2027 pre-Budget meeting chaired by President Anura Kumara Dissanayake at the Presidential Secretariat yesterday, according to the President\u2019s Media Division (PMD).\n\nThe PMD did not disclose further details on the proposed program, including its scope, the number of defence personnel expected to participate, the types of construction work envisaged or the budgetary implications.\n\nThe construction sector continues to face labour shortages, with the industry reporting a lack of skilled workers, increased competition for experienced tradespeople and higher employment costs. The sector is also grappling with higher procurement costs due to rising prices of imported construction materials and movements in global commodity prices.\n\nThe construction-sector proposal was among several areas considered during the meeting, which also focused on rationalising defence-sector research and development, technology adoption, overseas training and the operations of hospitals under the Ministry.\n\nPresident Dissanayake instructed officials to integrate the research and development centres currently operated separately by the Army, Navy and Air Force into a single national centre, arguing that the arrangement would enable their objectives to be achieved more efficiently.\n\nDiscussions also covered the rapid adoption of modern technology and advanced equipment by the defence sector, as well as increased allocations for overseas training to improve the professional skills and knowledge of defence personnel."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-08-18-dailyftlk-4579bd68",
    "num": "dailyftlk-4579bd68",
