    "chunk_size_bytes": 1803,
    "chunk_text": "Another mother in Weligama had left her one year old infant with her fourteen-year-old sister-in-law a few years ago under the pretext of visiting her husband and fled the area. In the same year, a disabled man had left his two sons aged four and five years at the Mahanama Bridge in Matara and had allegedly attempted to commit suicide by jumping into the Nilawala Ganga. According a similar report, two children were thrown into Kalu Ganga by their mother and were rescued by three army personnel on duty nearby.\n\nA sharp observer of child cruelty in the country would provide a long list of incidents of child abuse already published in the media, before they are forgotten.\n\nIn its latest classification of countries, the World Bank last month had elevated Sri Lanka from a lower middle-income country to an upper middle-income country, noting that Sri Lanka recorded real economic growth of 5% last year. Sri Lanka has crossed the threshold of per capita Gross National Income (GNI) of $ 4,636, though narrowly, to become upper middle-income country. The country has come out of bankruptcy, surpassing all macroeconomic goals set out by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) such as targets from exports, taxes and foreign employment.\n\nHowever, there is still a segment of population that is\u00a0 forced even to abandon their children or destroy them, not out of hatred but out of desperation. Poverty in the country is a chronic issue. Experts point out that \u201cSri Lanka is not inherently poor, but it faces severe economic stagnation and high poverty rates\u2014with over a quarter of the population affected\u2014largely due to the devastating 2022 debt and foreign currency crisis, decades of heavy borrowing, poorly managed state enterprises, and a heavy reliance on vulnerable traditional exports.\u201d"
