    "chunk_text": "The same weaknesses remain because there have not been major changes to the administrative system. Some officials may still occupy positions where they can obstruct reforms.\n\nA: Yes. If the Government wanted to retain existing officials, it could have established supervisory and advisory mechanisms to monitor performance against key result areas.\n\nUnfortunately, that has not happened sufficiently, and I am not satisfied with this aspect of the Government\u2019s performance.\n\nQ: Are you satisfied with the way the Government is exposing corruption and arresting people?\n\nA: Yes, but there are better ways of handling these cases without turning them into political spectacles. Legal procedures must be followed while ensuring efficiency.\n\nI am also concerned about delays in the Attorney General\u2019s Department and the judiciary. I am not blaming those institutions, but the system needs more staff and resources, improved procedures and clear timeframes.\n\nI do not agree that every arrest represents political revenge. The previous regime included many corrupt people, although there were also genuine individuals. If people who committed wrongdoing are brought to justice, that is appropriate.\n\nBut the process must be conducted properly. Ridiculing people in Parliament or publicly announcing that a case will be taken up within a week does not strengthen the justice system.\n\nQ: How do you assess the present Government\u2019s leadership under Anura Kumara Dissanayake?\n\nA: In general, people are happy with his performance, although I cannot comment fully because I do not know what is happening inside the Government. From the outside, however, I think its communication and coordination could improve.\n\nAt times, important leaders make different statements on the same issue. There should be greater consensus before positions are announced publicly."
